# Kaun Banega Arabpati 🎮

Remember those old DOS-based quiz games from back in the day? I found one from 2000 — "Kaun Banega Arabpati," basically a *Who Wants to Be a Millionaire?*-style game — sitting around in Turbo C++, using ancient graphics libraries that don't even run on modern computers anymore. Instead of letting it rot, I decided to rebuild it from scratch with a modern stack, and honestly it turned into a much bigger project than I expected.

This repo has three things in it:

- **`web/`** — the main version. A full browser-based quiz game with accounts, categories, a real KBC-style prize ladder, sound effects, voice narration, animations, the whole deal.
- **`native/`** — a portable C++17 console version of the same game, for anyone who wants to run it in a terminal without a browser.
- **`legacy/`** — the original 2000-era DOS source code, untouched, just kept here for history's sake.

## What's actually in the web version

- **Real accounts** — register, log in, log out. (Heads up: accounts are saved in your browser's local storage, not on a real server, so they won't follow you to a different device. That's a known trade-off for a project like this — no backend, no cost, works instantly.)
- **5 categories** to pick from — General Knowledge, Science, History, Entertainment, and a "Mixed" mode that shuffles questions from all of them together, just like the real show does.
- **~467 questions** in the pool, but every single game is still just 15 questions — the big pool just means you're less likely to see the same set twice.
- **The actual KBC prize ladder** — ₹1,000 up to ₹1,00,00,000, with the real checkpoint amounts at question 5 and question 10.
- **A 20-second timer** per question, because urgency makes trivia more fun.
- **Adaptive difficulty** — get a few right in a row without help, and it starts throwing harder stuff at you.
- **The three classic lifelines** — 50-50, Ask the Audience, and Phone a Friend (with a proper suspenseful pause before the friend answers, not an instant reply).
- **A dashboard and leaderboard** so you can track your best runs and see how you stack up against anyone else who's played on your browser.
- **Sound, voice narration, confetti, and a virtual host** who reacts to your answers — small touches, but they make it feel alive.
- **Hindi, English, and Urdu** support for all the menus and buttons. (The actual quiz questions stay in English everywhere — translating hundreds of trivia questions accurately is its own project, and I'd rather ship it correctly in one language than sloppily in three.)
- **Installable as an app** (PWA) if you serve it through a local server instead of just opening the file directly — works offline once installed.

## Running it

**Web version** — just open `web/index.html` in a browser. That's it, no setup.

If you want the installable/offline features to actually work, you need to serve it properly instead of double-clicking the file:
```bash
cd web
python3 -m http.server 8000
# then visit http://localhost:8000
```

**Native C++ version** — needs a C++17 compiler:
```bash
cd native
g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -o kbap kbap.cpp
./kbap
```

## A quick honest note

The account system in this project is intentionally simple — everything lives in browser storage, and passwords are only lightly obfuscated, not properly hashed the way a real production app would do it. This was a deliberate choice to keep the project running without needing a server, a database, or any ongoing cost. If I ever turn this into something more serious, a real backend is the obvious next step.

## Credit where it's due

The original 2000 DOS game and its questions were made by **Jayant Kumar Gandhi**. That source is preserved as-is in [`legacy/`](./legacy). Everything else in this repo — the modern rewrite, the new features, the ~467 new trivia questions, the design — is my own work, built from the ground up.